---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { LINKEDIN_URL, SCHEDULING_LINK } from '../consts';
---

<MarketingLayout
	title="Work | People Purpose Product"
	description="Three ways to fill the product owner seat on a funded mission-driven team: a product studio, a fractional seat, and coaching for the person already doing the job."
>
	<section class="wrap">
		<div class="measure page-header">
			<h1>Work</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				You know your impact space better than any consultant will. You have funding, and
				now you can go build without waiting on a hire. That part is new, and it's good.
			</p>
			<p class="lede">
				Backlogs and milestones are the easy part. The hard part is deciding where to
				invest, and moving before you have all the information you'd like. That's the
				gap. Here are three ways to close it, depending on what you already have.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap services">
		<div class="label ruled">Three ways to work together</div>

		<article class="service">
			<div class="service-head">
				<h2>Product studio</h2>
				<p class="fit"><b>Fits when</b> you have funding and a real problem, and nobody inside to run the build.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="service-body">
				<p>
					You get a working product, and a team that can tell you why it was built this way
					and not some other way. I own the roadmap and bring the engineers.
				</p>
			</div>
		</article>

		<article class="service">
			<div class="service-head">
				<h2>Fractional product owner</h2>
				<p class="fit"><b>Fits when</b> you already have engineers, and nobody deciding what they build.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="service-body">
				<p>
					The roadmap stays yours. I take the product owner seat on your team and sit in the
					meetings: drafting the requirements, designing the experiments, reading what comes
					back, making the call with your people in the room.
				</p>
				<p>
					A product manager who runs the product day to day is table stakes. The part that's
					harder to find is ownership: deciding what gets built, in what order, and standing
					behind the call. I do both.
				</p>
			</div>
		</article>

		<article class="service">
			<div class="service-head">
				<h2>Product owner coaching</h2>
				<p class="fit"><b>Fits when</b> someone on your team has the instincts and has never had anyone to learn the craft from.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="service-body">
				<p>
					For the person already doing this job without the title. We draft the roadmap
					together, run the experiments together, and sit in it together when an idea
					flops. Then you keep doing it after I'm gone.
				</p>
			</div>
		</article>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap">
		<div class="measure how">
			<h2>How we work</h2>
			<p>
				I can do it for you. I can teach you how. What I want is to do it with you: draft
				the roadmap together, run the experiment, be uncomfortable together and watch our
				ideas flop.
			</p>
			<p>
				The flopping is the part people skip, and it's the part that pays. A client wanted a
				chatbot to handle one step of their flow. We looked at it together and
				realized a plain form would do that same job correctly every single time. The
				chatbot's real use turned out to be telling us which steps should have been forms
				all along. That's a smaller product than the one we started with, and a better one,
				and no amount of prompting would have gotten us there.
			</p>
			<p>
				Every engagement ends the same way. Your team can do this without me.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap">
		<div class="measure closing">
			<h2>Start a conversation</h2>
			<p>
				If any of this resonates, I'd love to talk. Book 30 minutes and tell me what
				you're working on.
			</p>
			<div class="cta-row">
				<a class="primary" href={SCHEDULING_LINK} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Find a time &rarr;</a>
				<span class="aside">Prefer email? Write me at <a class="email-link" href={LINKEDIN_URL}>hello at this domain</a>.</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</MarketingLayout>

<script>
	// Assemble the address in the browser so scrapers reading the raw HTML
	// never see a complete email. The href above is a fallback (LinkedIn)
	// for the rare no-JS visitor.
	const user = 'hello';
	const domain = 'peoplepurposeproduct.com';
	const address = `${user}@${domain}`;
	document.querySelectorAll('.email-link').forEach((el) => {
		el.setAttribute('href', `mailto:${address}`);
		el.textContent = address;
	});
</script>

<style>
	.page-header {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 24px;
		padding: 96px 0 56px;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 22px;
		line-height: 1.45;
		color: var(--body);
	}

	.services {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		padding-bottom: 32px;
	}
	.service {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 250px minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: 40px;
		align-items: start;
		padding: 26px 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-light);
	}
	.service-head {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 6px;
	}
	.service-head h2 {
		font-size: 26px;
		line-height: 1.2;
	}
	.fit {
		font-size: 16px;
		line-height: 1.45;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.service-body {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 12px;
	}
	.service-body p {
		font-size: 18px;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--body);
	}

	.how {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 18px;
		padding: 32px 0 56px;
	}
	.how p,
	.closing p {
		font-size: 19px;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--body);
	}

	.closing {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 18px;
		padding: 48px 0 72px;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.page-header {
			padding: 48px 0 40px;
			gap: 18px;
		}
		.lede {
			font-size: 20px;
		}
		.service {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: 14px;
			padding: 22px 0;
		}
		.service-head h2 {
			font-size: 24px;
		}
		.how {
			padding: 24px 0 48px;
		}
		.closing {
			padding: 40px 0 56px;
		}
	}
</style>
